IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `oapa-nouns`
IMPORT `oapa-homicide`
IMPORT `oapa-threats`
IMPORT `oapa-danger-bodily-harm`
IMPORT `oapa-danger-neglect-explosives`
IMPORT `oapa-danger-railways-vehicles`
IMPORT `oapa-assaults`
IMPORT `oapa-bigamy`
IMPORT `oapa-abortion-birth`
IMPORT `oapa-gunpowder`
IMPORT `oapa-other`
IMPORT `oapa-table`

-- Offences against the Person Act 1861 (24 & 25 Vict. c. 100) -- LAYER 2: THE SEAT.
--
-- Text encoded: the revised text on legislation.gov.uk at the point in time 2026-04-29, as deposited in
-- ukpga-Vict-24-25-100.txt (a plain-text rendering by a converter script; it is not the official text).
--
-- SEAT: a person who may be accused of an offence under the Act (the person suspected or charged), asking what the Act
-- makes of what they did or are alleged to have done, and the most that can follow.
--
-- This layer phrases the rules of the modules imported above as the questions that person would ask. Nothing here is
-- new law: each question builds the layer-1 record from the person's answers, in its WHERE, and calls the layer-1 rule.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS. Every question takes plain inputs (yes/no, numbers, and the modules' own choices), one for each fact the
-- person can state, each with an @desc worded as the person would be asked. A record field that the called rule never reads
-- is given a fixed placeholder, with a comment saying so; the placeholders are listed in NOTES.md.
--
-- WHAT A QUESTION RETURNS. An offence question returns `What the Act makes of it`: whether the facts make out the offence,
-- what the Act calls it, how the Act says it is tried, and the most the Act states can follow. Wherever a term of a sentence
-- is hidden by ". . ." in the deposited text, the answer says "a term the text does not state"; it never fills a gap.
-- The Act has no schedule or table; the three layer-1 statements for each offence are joined in oapa-table.l4.
--
-- THE JUSTICES, THE COURT AND THE POLICE get no seat. What they may do to the person, or must give the person, appears as a
-- question about the person's own position: s 42 (what two justices may order), s 44 (the certificate owed), s 45 (the
-- protection that follows), s 65 (the search warrant).

DECLARE `What the Act makes of it` HAS
    `the facts make out the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `what the Act calls it` IS A STRING
    `how the Act says it is tried` IS A STRING
    `the most the Act states can follow` IS A STRING

GIVEN t IS A Term
GIVETH A STRING
`the term in words` t MEANS
    CONSIDER t
    WHEN `for life` THEN "for life"
    WHEN `for a term not exceeding` m THEN CONCAT "for a term not exceeding ", TOSTRING m, " months"
    WHEN `for a term the text does not state` THEN "for a term the text does not state"

GIVEN s IS A Sentence
GIVETH A STRING
`the sentence in words` s MEANS
    CONSIDER s
    WHEN `penal servitude` t THEN CONCAT "penal servitude ", `the term in words` t
    WHEN `imprisonment` t THEN CONCAT "imprisonment ", `the term in words` t
    WHEN `imprisonment in default of paying a fine and costs` t THEN CONCAT "imprisonment in default of paying a fine and costs ", `the term in words` t
    WHEN `a fine, with any costs, not exceeding` p THEN CONCAT "a fine, with any costs, not exceeding ", TOSTRING p, " pounds"

GIVEN xs IS A LIST OF Sentence
GIVETH A STRING
`the sentences in words` xs MEANS
    CONSIDER xs
    WHEN EMPTY THEN "no sentence is stated"
    WHEN y FOLLOWED BY EMPTY THEN `the sentence in words` y
    WHEN y FOLLOWED BY rest THEN CONCAT `the sentence in words` y, "; ", `the sentences in words` rest

GIVEN c IS A Classification
GIVETH A STRING
`the classification in words` c MEANS
    CONSIDER c
    WHEN felony THEN "a felony"
    WHEN misdemeanor THEN "a misdemeanor"
    WHEN `an offence, with no further classification` THEN "an offence (the section uses no other word)"
    WHEN `no classification stated` THEN "not classified as a felony or a misdemeanor by the text"

GIVEN m IS A `Mode of trial`
GIVETH A STRING
`the mode of trial in words` m MEANS
    CONSIDER m
    WHEN `on indictment` THEN "on indictment"
    WHEN `summarily before justices of the peace` THEN "summarily, before two justices of the peace"
    WHEN `the text does not say` THEN "the text does not say"

GIVEN `made out` IS A BOOLEAN
      c IS A Classification
      m IS A `Mode of trial`
      ss IS A LIST OF Sentence
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
`the Act's answer` `made out` c m ss MEANS
    IF `made out`
    THEN `What the Act makes of it` WITH
            `the facts make out the offence` IS TRUE
            `what the Act calls it` IS `the classification in words` c
            `how the Act says it is tried` IS `the mode of trial in words` m
            `the most the Act states can follow` IS `the sentences in words` ss
    ELSE `What the Act makes of it` WITH
            `the facts make out the offence` IS FALSE
            `what the Act calls it` IS "Not made out on the facts you gave."
            `how the Act says it is tried` IS "Not applicable."
            `the most the Act states can follow` IS "Nothing follows from this section on the facts you gave."

-- The common case: the Act's own three statements for an offence, looked up in oapa-table.l4, given the rule's verdict.
GIVEN `made out` IS A BOOLEAN
      o IS AN Offence
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
`what the Act makes of an offence` `made out` o MEANS
    `the Act's answer` `made out`
                       (`the text calls the offence` o)
                       (`the text says the offence is tried` o)
                       (`the sentences the text states for the offence` o)

§ `Offences against the Person Act 1861 -- the questions of a person who may be accused of an offence`


-- Situation-only group (sections 4 to 10): the form may ask "Has a person died, or are you accused of
-- trying to bring about a killing?" before showing it.
§§ `Murder, manslaughter and killing abroad`

-- s 4. The five verbs are alternatives; what was put to the other person must have been a murder.
-- Whether the other person is a subject, or is within the dominions, does not matter.
@export Could what I said to someone amount to soliciting or proposing a murder (s 4)?
GIVEN `I solicited someone to murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you solicit someone to murder another person?
      `I encouraged someone to murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you encourage someone to murder another person?
      `I persuaded someone to murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you persuade someone to murder another person?
      `I tried to persuade someone to murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you try (endeavour) to persuade someone to murder another person?
      `I proposed to someone that they murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you propose to someone that they murder another person?
      `what I put to them would be a murder` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the killing you put to them have been a murder?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could what I said amount to soliciting or proposing a murder` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 4: soliciting or proposing a murder` c) `s 4: soliciting or proposing a murder`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 4` WITH
            `solicited a person to murder another person` IS `I solicited someone to murder another person`
            `encouraged a person to murder another person` IS `I encouraged someone to murder another person`
            `persuaded a person to murder another person` IS `I persuaded someone to murder another person`
            `endeavoured to persuade a person to murder another person` IS `I tried to persuade someone to murder another person`
            `proposed to a person that they murder another person` IS `I proposed to someone that they murder another person`
            `what was put to that person would be a murder` IS `what I put to them would be a murder`
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 4 says it does not matter whether the other person is a subject of Her Majesty.
            `the other person is a subject of Her Majesty` IS TRUE
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 4 says it does not matter whether the other person is within the Queen's dominions.
            `the other person is within the Queen's dominions` IS TRUE

-- ss 1 and 2 (blank in this text) and s 5. The deposited text does not define murder, and
-- section 5 gives only the penalty for manslaughter, not its elements. The answer is the
-- explicit value `the deposited text does not say`; it is never a guess.
@export Does this text tell me whether what I did is a murder (ss 1, 2)?
GIVETH A `What the Act says`
DECIDE `Does this text tell me whether what I did is a murder` IS
    `whether what happened is a murder, as this text defines murder`

@export Does this text tell me whether what I did is a manslaughter (s 5)?
GIVETH A `What the Act says`
DECIDE `Does this text tell me whether what I did is a manslaughter` IS
    `whether what happened is a manslaughter, as this text defines manslaughter`

-- s 5. "Every person who shall be convicted of manslaughter": the penalty only.
@export What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of manslaughter (s 5)?
GIVEN `I have been convicted of manslaughter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been convicted of manslaughter?
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of manslaughter` IS
    IF `I have been convicted of manslaughter`
    THEN `the sentences in words` (`s 5: the sentences the text states for a conviction of manslaughter` TRUE)
    ELSE "Nothing follows under section 5 until you have been convicted of manslaughter."

-- s 9. A subject of Her Majesty who commits murder or manslaughter on land out of the United Kingdom
-- may be dealt with, indicted, tried and punished in any place in England or Ireland.
@export Can I be dealt with, tried and punished in England or Ireland for a killing abroad (s 9)?
GIVEN `what happened is a murder or a manslaughter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is what you are accused of a murder or a manslaughter?
      `it happened on land outside the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did it happen on land outside the United Kingdom?
      `I am a subject of Her Majesty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a subject of Her Majesty?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be tried in England or Ireland for a killing abroad` IS
    `s 9: the person may be dealt with, tried and punished in England or Ireland` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Killing abroad under section 9` WITH
            `a murder or manslaughter was committed` IS `what happened is a murder or a manslaughter`
            `on land out of the United Kingdom` IS `it happened on land outside the United Kingdom`
            `the person charged is a subject of Her Majesty` IS `I am a subject of Her Majesty`
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 9 applies whether the place is within the Queen's dominions or not.
            `the place is within the Queen's dominions` IS TRUE
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 9 applies whether the person killed was a subject of Her Majesty or not.
            `the person killed was a subject of Her Majesty` IS TRUE
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; it is read only by the proviso below.
            `the killing was out of England or Ireland` IS TRUE

-- s 9, proviso. The Act does not prevent a trial out of England or Ireland for a killing that took place there.
-- This is the other side of the liability above: it says what the Act does not take away.
@export Does the Act leave it open to try me outside England and Ireland for a killing abroad (s 9)?
GIVEN `what happened is a murder or a manslaughter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is what you are accused of a murder or a manslaughter?
      `the killing was outside England and Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the killing take place outside England and Ireland?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act leave it open to try me outside England and Ireland for a killing abroad` IS
    `s 9: a trial out of England or Ireland is not prevented by this Act` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Killing abroad under section 9` WITH
            `a murder or manslaughter was committed` IS `what happened is a murder or a manslaughter`
            -- Placeholder: the proviso rule does not read this field.
            `on land out of the United Kingdom` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: the proviso rule does not read this field.
            `the person charged is a subject of Her Majesty` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: the proviso rule does not read this field.
            `the place is within the Queen's dominions` IS TRUE
            -- Placeholder: the proviso rule does not read this field.
            `the person killed was a subject of Her Majesty` IS TRUE
            `the killing was out of England or Ireland` IS `the killing was outside England and Ireland`

-- s 10. A person criminally hurt in one place who dies in another, where the hurt was on the sea or out of England and Ireland
-- and the death in England or Ireland (or the other way round), may be dealt with, tried and punished in England or Ireland.
@export Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places (s 10)?
GIVEN `I criminally hurt a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you accused of criminally striking, poisoning or otherwise hurting a person?
      `where the person was hurt` IS A `Place` @desc Where was the person hurt?
      `the person died of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person die of that stroke, poisoning or hurt?
      `where the person died` IS A `Place` @desc Where did the person die?
      `it amounts to murder or manslaughter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does what happened amount to murder or manslaughter?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` IS
    `s 10: the person may be dealt with, tried and punished in England or Ireland` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Hurt and death under section 10` WITH
            `a person was criminally stricken, poisoned or otherwise hurt` IS `I criminally hurt a person`
            `where the person was hurt` IS `where the person was hurt`
            `the person died of that stroke, poisoning or hurt` IS `the person died of it`
            `where the person died` IS `where the person died`
            `the offence amounts to murder or manslaughter` IS `it amounts to murder or manslaughter`

§§ `Threats to kill`

-- s 16. A threat to kill, made without lawful excuse, intending the other person to fear it will be carried out.
-- Whether the person meant to carry it out is not an element.
@export Could a threat I made amount to the offence of threatening to kill (s 16)?
GIVEN `I made a threat to someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you make a threat to another person?
      `I had a lawful excuse for making it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a lawful excuse for making the threat?
      `I meant them to fear it would be carried out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you mean the other person to fear that the threat would be carried out?
      `the threat was to kill them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the threat to kill the person you made it to?
      `the threat was to kill someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the threat to kill a third person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could a threat I made amount to the offence of threatening to kill` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 16: threats to kill` c) `s 16: threats to kill`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 16` WITH
            `made a threat to another person` IS `I made a threat to someone`
            `had a lawful excuse for making it` IS `I had a lawful excuse for making it`
            `intended that the other person would fear it would be carried out` IS `I meant them to fear it would be carried out`
            `the threat was to kill the other person` IS `the threat was to kill them`
            `the threat was to kill a third person` IS `the threat was to kill someone else`
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 16 does not make an intention to carry the threat out an element.
            `meant to carry the threat out` IS FALSE

§§ `Endangering life and causing bodily harm`

-- s 17. Unlawfully and maliciously preventing or impeding a person trying to save their life from a ship or vessel in distress,
-- wrecked, stranded or cast on shore, or trying to save the life of such a person.
@export Could I have broken section 17 by stopping someone saving their life from a shipwreck?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I stopped or impeded someone saving their own life from a ship in distress` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you prevent or impede a person on board, or who had left, a ship or vessel in distress, wrecked, stranded or cast on shore, in trying to save their own life?
      `I stopped or impeded someone saving the life of such a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you prevent or impede a person in trying to save the life of such a person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 17 by stopping someone saving their life from a shipwreck` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 17: impeding a person endeavouring to save their life from shipwreck` c) `s 17: impeding a person trying to save their life from shipwreck`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 17` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `prevented or impeded a person on board or who had quitted a ship or vessel in distress, wrecked, stranded or cast on shore, in their endeavour to save their life` IS `I stopped or impeded someone saving their own life from a ship in distress`
            `prevented or impeded a person in their endeavour to save the life of such a person` IS `I stopped or impeded someone saving the life of such a person`

-- s 18. Unlawfully and maliciously wounding or causing grievous bodily harm with intent to do grievous bodily harm,
-- or to resist or prevent lawful apprehension or detainer.
@export Could I have broken section 18 by wounding or harming someone with intent?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I wounded someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you wound a person?
      `I caused someone grievous bodily harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you cause grievous bodily harm to a person?
      `I meant to do them grievous bodily harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to do some grievous bodily harm to a person?
      `I meant to resist or prevent a lawful arrest or detention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of a person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 18 by wounding or harming someone with intent` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 18: wounding or causing grievous bodily harm, with intent` c) `s 18: wounding or causing grievous bodily harm, with intent`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 18` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `wounded a person` IS `I wounded someone`
            `caused grievous bodily harm to a person` IS `I caused someone grievous bodily harm`
            `intended to do some grievous bodily harm to a person` IS `I meant to do them grievous bodily harm`
            `intended to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of a person` IS `I meant to resist or prevent a lawful arrest or detention`

-- s 20. Unlawfully and maliciously wounding or inflicting grievous bodily harm, with or without a weapon or instrument.
@export Could I have broken section 20 by wounding or inflicting grievous bodily harm?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I wounded another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you wound another person?
      `I inflicted grievous bodily harm on another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you inflict grievous bodily harm on another person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 20 by wounding or inflicting grievous bodily harm` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 20: wounding or inflicting grievous bodily harm` c) `s 20: wounding or inflicting grievous bodily harm`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 20` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `wounded another person` IS `I wounded another person`
            `inflicted grievous bodily harm upon another person` IS `I inflicted grievous bodily harm on another person`
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 20 applies "with or without any weapon or instrument".
            `used a weapon or instrument` IS FALSE

-- s 21. Attempting to choke, suffocate or strangle, or by such means to render insensible, with intent to enable oneself or another
-- to commit, or to assist another in committing, an indictable offence.
@export Could I have broken section 21 by attempting to choke someone to commit an indictable offence?
GIVEN `I tried to choke, suffocate or strangle someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you attempt to choke, suffocate or strangle another person?
      `I tried by such means to make someone insensible or unable to resist` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you attempt, by means calculated to choke, suffocate or strangle, to render another person insensible, unconscious or incapable of resistance?
      `I meant to enable myself or someone else to commit an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend by this to enable yourself or another person to commit an indictable offence?
      `I meant to help someone else to commit an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend by this to assist another person in committing an indictable offence?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 21 by attempting to choke someone to commit an indictable offence` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 21: attempting to choke or render insensible, to commit an indictable offence` c) `s 21: attempting to choke or render insensible, to commit an indictable offence`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 21` WITH
            `attempted to choke, suffocate or strangle another person` IS `I tried to choke, suffocate or strangle someone`
            `attempted, by means calculated to choke, suffocate or strangle, to render another person insensible, unconscious or incapable of resistance` IS `I tried by such means to make someone insensible or unable to resist`
            `intended thereby to enable themselves or another person to commit an indictable offence` IS `I meant to enable myself or someone else to commit an indictable offence`
            `intended thereby to assist another person in committing an indictable offence` IS `I meant to help someone else to commit an indictable offence`

-- s 22. Unlawfully applying or administering, or attempting to apply or administer, a stupefying or overpowering drug, matter or thing,
-- with intent to enable oneself or another to commit, or to assist another in committing, an indictable offence.
@export Could I have broken section 22 by giving someone a stupefying drug to commit an indictable offence?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `I gave someone a stupefying drug` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you apply or administer to a person, or cause a person to take, a stupefying or overpowering drug, matter or thing?
      `I tried to give someone a stupefying drug` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you attempt to apply or administer such a thing to a person, or to cause a person to take it?
      `I meant to enable myself or someone else to commit an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend by this to enable yourself or another person to commit an indictable offence?
      `I meant to help someone else to commit an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend by this to assist another person in committing an indictable offence?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 22 by giving someone a stupefying drug to commit an indictable offence` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 22: administering a stupefying drug, to commit an indictable offence` c) `s 22: administering a stupefying drug, to commit an indictable offence`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 22` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `applied or administered to a person, or caused to be taken by a person, a stupefying or overpowering drug, matter or thing` IS `I gave someone a stupefying drug`
            `attempted to apply or administer to a person, or to cause to be taken by a person, a stupefying or overpowering drug, matter or thing` IS `I tried to give someone a stupefying drug`
            `intended thereby to enable themselves or another person to commit an indictable offence` IS `I meant to enable myself or someone else to commit an indictable offence`
            `intended thereby to assist another person in committing an indictable offence` IS `I meant to help someone else to commit an indictable offence`

-- s 23. Unlawfully and maliciously administering, or causing to be administered or taken, a poison or other destructive or noxious thing,
-- so as thereby to endanger life or inflict grievous bodily harm.
@export Could I have broken section 23 by giving someone poison that endangered their life or caused grievous bodily harm?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I gave someone, or caused them to take, a poison or noxious thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you administer to another person, or cause to be administered to or taken by another person, a poison or other destructive or noxious thing?
      `it endangered their life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did it endanger that person's life?
      `it inflicted grievous bodily harm on them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did it inflict grievous bodily harm on that person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 23 by giving someone poison that endangered their life or caused grievous bodily harm` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 23: administering poison so as to endanger life or inflict grievous bodily harm` c) `s 23: administering poison so as to endanger life or inflict grievous bodily harm`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 23` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `administered to another person, or caused to be administered to or taken by another person, a poison or other destructive or noxious thing` IS `I gave someone, or caused them to take, a poison or noxious thing`
            `thereby endangered the life of that person` IS `it endangered their life`
            `thereby inflicted grievous bodily harm upon that person` IS `it inflicted grievous bodily harm on them`

-- s 24. Unlawfully and maliciously administering, or causing to be administered or taken, a poison or other destructive or noxious thing,
-- with intent to injure, aggrieve or annoy.
@export Could I have broken section 24 by giving someone poison with intent to injure, aggrieve or annoy?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I gave someone, or caused them to take, a poison or noxious thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you administer to another person, or cause to be administered to or taken by another person, a poison or other destructive or noxious thing?
      `I meant to injure them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to injure that person?
      `I meant to aggrieve them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to aggrieve that person?
      `I meant to annoy them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to annoy that person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 24 by giving someone poison with intent to injure, aggrieve or annoy` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 24: administering poison with intent to injure, aggrieve or annoy` c) `s 24: administering poison with intent to injure, aggrieve or annoy`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 24` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `administered to another person, or caused to be administered to or taken by another person, a poison or other destructive or noxious thing` IS `I gave someone, or caused them to take, a poison or noxious thing`
            `intended to injure that person` IS `I meant to injure them`
            `intended to aggrieve that person` IS `I meant to aggrieve them`
            `intended to annoy that person` IS `I meant to annoy them`

-- s 25. A person tried for the section 23 felony may, if the jury is not satisfied of the felony but is
-- satisfied of the section 24 misdemeanor, be found guilty of the misdemeanor, and is punished as for it.
-- The jury decides; the person states what happened at the trial.
@export If I am tried under section 23 and the jury is not satisfied of that, can I be found guilty under section 24 instead (s 25)?
GIVEN `I am tried for the section 23 felony` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you being tried for the section 23 felony (administering poison so as to endanger life or inflict grievous bodily harm)?
      `the jury is not satisfied I am guilty of that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the jury not satisfied that you are guilty of that felony?
      `the jury is satisfied I am guilty of the section 24 misdemeanor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the jury satisfied that you are guilty of the section 24 misdemeanor (administering poison with intent to injure, aggrieve or annoy)?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `If I am tried under section 23 can I be found guilty under section 24 instead` IS
    `the Act's answer` (`s 25: the jury may acquit of the section 23 felony and find the person guilty of the section 24 misdemeanor` t)
                       (`the text calls the offence` `s 24: administering poison with intent to injure, aggrieve or annoy`)
                       `the text does not say`
                       (`s 25: the sentences open after an alternative verdict` t)
    WHERE
        t MEANS `Trial under section 25` WITH
            `the person is tried for the felony in section 23` IS `I am tried for the section 23 felony`
            `the jury is not satisfied that the person is guilty of that felony` IS `the jury is not satisfied I am guilty of that`
            `the jury is satisfied that the person is guilty of the misdemeanor in section 24` IS `the jury is satisfied I am guilty of the section 24 misdemeanor`

§§ `Neglect, abandonment, explosives and traps`

-- s 26. A master or mistress legally liable to provide necessary food, clothing or lodging who wilfully and without lawful excuse
-- refuses or neglects to, or who unlawfully and maliciously does bodily harm, so that life is endangered or health permanently injured.
-- See NOTES.md fork 26a: the result clause governs both limbs.
@export Could I have broken section 26 by neglecting or harming my apprentice or servant?
GIVEN `I am legally liable to provide my apprentice or servant with necessary food, clothing or lodging` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you legally liable, as a master or mistress, to provide your apprentice or servant with necessary food, clothing or lodging?
      `I wilfully refused or neglected to provide it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you wilfully refuse or neglect to provide it?
      `I had a lawful excuse for refusing or neglecting` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a lawful excuse for refusing or neglecting?
      `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I did, or caused, bodily harm to my apprentice or servant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you do, or cause to be done, bodily harm to your apprentice or servant?
      `their life was endangered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the life of the apprentice or servant endangered?
      `their health has been or is likely to be permanently injured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the health of the apprentice or servant been, or is it likely to be, permanently injured?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 26 by neglecting or harming my apprentice or servant` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 26: neglecting or harming an apprentice or servant` c) `s 26: neglecting or harming an apprentice or servant`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 26` WITH
            `legally liable, as a master or mistress, to provide an apprentice or servant with necessary food, clothing or lodging` IS `I am legally liable to provide my apprentice or servant with necessary food, clothing or lodging`
            `wilfully refused or neglected to provide it` IS `I wilfully refused or neglected to provide it`
            `had a lawful excuse for refusing or neglecting` IS `I had a lawful excuse for refusing or neglecting`
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `did or caused to be done bodily harm to the apprentice or servant` IS `I did, or caused, bodily harm to my apprentice or servant`
            `the life of the apprentice or servant was endangered` IS `their life was endangered`
            `the health of the apprentice or servant has been or is likely to be permanently injured` IS `their health has been or is likely to be permanently injured`

-- s 27. Unlawfully abandoning or exposing a child under the age of two years, so that its life is endangered or its health
-- permanently injured. Two years is 24 complete months: 23 is under, 24 is not.
@export Could I have broken section 27 by abandoning or exposing a child under two years old?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `I abandoned or exposed a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you abandon or expose a child?
      `the age of the child in complete months` IS A NUMBER @desc How old was the child, in complete months?
      `the child's life was endangered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the life of the child endangered?
      `the child's health has been or is likely to be permanently injured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the health of the child been, or is it likely to be, permanently injured?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 27 by abandoning or exposing a child under two years old` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 27: abandoning or exposing a child under two years` c) `s 27: abandoning or exposing a child under two years`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 27` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `abandoned or exposed a child` IS `I abandoned or exposed a child`
            `the age of the child in complete months` IS `the age of the child in complete months`
            `the life of the child was endangered` IS `the child's life was endangered`
            `the health of the child has been or is likely to be permanently injured` IS `the child's health has been or is likely to be permanently injured`

-- s 28. Unlawfully and maliciously, by the explosion of gunpowder or other explosive substance, burning, maiming, disfiguring,
-- disabling or doing grievous bodily harm to a person.
@export Could I have broken section 28 by causing bodily injury with an explosion?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `it was done by an explosion of gunpowder or another explosive substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it done by the explosion of gunpowder or another explosive substance?
      `I burnt someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you burn a person?
      `I maimed someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you maim a person?
      `I disfigured someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you disfigure a person?
      `I disabled someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you disable a person?
      `I did someone grievous bodily harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you do grievous bodily harm to a person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 28 by causing bodily injury with an explosion` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 28: causing bodily injury by an explosion` c) `s 28: causing bodily injury by an explosion`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 28` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `by the explosion of gunpowder or other explosive substance` IS `it was done by an explosion of gunpowder or another explosive substance`
            `burnt a person` IS `I burnt someone`
            `maimed a person` IS `I maimed someone`
            `disfigured a person` IS `I disfigured someone`
            `disabled a person` IS `I disabled someone`
            `did grievous bodily harm to a person` IS `I did someone grievous bodily harm`

-- s 29. Unlawfully and maliciously causing gunpowder or another explosive substance to explode, sending or delivering an explosive
-- or dangerous thing, putting or laying a corrosive fluid or explosive, or casting or applying one to a person, with intent to burn,
-- maim, disfigure or disable, or to do grievous bodily harm. Whether bodily injury was effected does not matter.
@export Could I have broken section 29 by causing an explosion, or sending or applying a dangerous thing, with intent?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I caused gunpowder or another explosive substance to explode` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you cause gunpowder or another explosive substance to explode?
      `I sent or delivered an explosive or dangerous thing to someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you send or deliver to a person, or cause a person to take or receive, an explosive substance or other dangerous or noxious thing?
      `I put or laid a corrosive fluid or explosive at a place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you put or lay at a place a corrosive fluid or a destructive or explosive substance?
      `I cast or applied a corrosive fluid or explosive to someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you cast or throw at or upon, or otherwise apply to, a person a corrosive fluid or a destructive or explosive substance?
      `I meant to burn, maim, disfigure or disable someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to burn, maim, disfigure or disable a person?
      `I meant to do someone grievous bodily harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to do some grievous bodily harm to a person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 29 by causing an explosion or sending or applying a dangerous thing with intent` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 29: causing an explosion, or sending or applying a dangerous thing, with intent` c) `s 29: causing an explosion, or sending or applying a dangerous thing, with intent`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 29` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `caused gunpowder or another explosive substance to explode` IS `I caused gunpowder or another explosive substance to explode`
            `sent or delivered to a person, or caused to be taken or received by a person, an explosive substance or other dangerous or noxious thing` IS `I sent or delivered an explosive or dangerous thing to someone`
            `put or laid at a place a corrosive fluid or a destructive or explosive substance` IS `I put or laid a corrosive fluid or explosive at a place`
            `cast or threw at or upon, or otherwise applied to, a person a corrosive fluid or a destructive or explosive substance` IS `I cast or applied a corrosive fluid or explosive to someone`
            `intended to burn, maim, disfigure or disable a person` IS `I meant to burn, maim, disfigure or disable someone`
            `intended to do some grievous bodily harm to a person` IS `I meant to do someone grievous bodily harm`
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 29 applies "whether any bodily injury be effected or not".
            `bodily injury was effected` IS FALSE

-- s 30. Unlawfully and maliciously placing or throwing gunpowder or another explosive substance in, into, upon, against or near a building,
-- ship or vessel, with intent to do bodily injury to a person. Whether an explosion took place or injury was effected does not matter.
@export Could I have broken section 30 by placing an explosive near a building, ship or vessel with intent?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I placed or threw gunpowder or another explosive substance in, into, upon, against or near a building, ship or vessel` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you place or throw in, into, upon, against or near a building, ship or vessel gunpowder or another explosive substance?
      `I meant to do bodily injury to someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to do bodily injury to a person?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 30 by placing an explosive near a building, ship or vessel with intent` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 30: placing an explosive near a building, ship or vessel, with intent` c) `s 30: placing an explosive near a building, ship or vessel, with intent`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 30` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `placed or threw in, into, upon, against or near a building, ship or vessel gunpowder or another explosive substance` IS `I placed or threw gunpowder or another explosive substance in, into, upon, against or near a building, ship or vessel`
            `intended to do bodily injury to a person` IS `I meant to do bodily injury to someone`
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 30 applies "whether or not any explosion take place".
            `an explosion took place` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 30 applies "whether or not any bodily injury be effected".
            `bodily injury was effected` IS FALSE

-- s 31. Setting or placing, or causing to be set or placed, or knowingly and wilfully letting continue, a spring gun, man trap or other engine
-- calculated to destroy human life or inflict grievous bodily harm, with the stated intent. A gin or trap usually set to destroy vermin is
-- excepted, and so is an engine set from sunset to sunrise in a dwelling house for its protection. See NOTES.md fork 31a.
@export Could I have broken section 31 by setting a spring gun, man trap or similar engine?
GIVEN `it is a spring gun, man trap or other engine calculated to destroy human life or inflict grievous bodily harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a spring gun, man trap or other engine calculated to destroy human life or inflict grievous bodily harm?
      `I set or placed it, or caused it to be set or placed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you set or place it, or cause it to be set or placed?
      `I meant it to destroy or harm a trespasser or anyone coming in contact with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend that it should destroy or inflict grievous bodily harm on a trespasser or other person coming in contact with it?
      `I meant it to be set so that it may destroy or harm anyone coming in contact with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend it to be set so that it may destroy or inflict grievous bodily harm on a trespasser or other person coming in contact with it?
      `I knowingly and wilfully let it continue set where I was in possession or occupation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you knowingly and wilfully let it continue set or placed, by someone else, in a place then or afterwards in your possession or occupation?
      `it is a gin or trap of the kind usually set to destroy vermin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a gin or trap of the kind usually set or placed with the intent of destroying vermin?
      `it was set from sunset to sunrise in a dwelling house to protect it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it set, placed or continued from sunset to sunrise in a dwelling house for the protection of the dwelling house?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 31 by setting a spring gun, man trap or similar engine` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 31: setting a spring gun or man trap` c) `s 31: setting a spring gun or man trap`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 31` WITH
            `the engine is a spring gun, man trap or other engine calculated to destroy human life or inflict grievous bodily harm` IS `it is a spring gun, man trap or other engine calculated to destroy human life or inflict grievous bodily harm`
            `set or placed it, or caused it to be set or placed` IS `I set or placed it, or caused it to be set or placed`
            `intended that it should destroy or inflict grievous bodily harm upon a trespasser or other person coming in contact with it` IS `I meant it to destroy or harm a trespasser or anyone coming in contact with it`
            `intended it to be set so that it may destroy or inflict grievous bodily harm upon a trespasser or other person coming in contact with it` IS `I meant it to be set so that it may destroy or harm anyone coming in contact with it`
            `knowingly and wilfully permitted it, set or placed by some other person in a place then in or afterwards coming into the person's possession or occupation, to continue so set or placed` IS `I knowingly and wilfully let it continue set where I was in possession or occupation`
            `it is a gin or trap of the kind usually set or placed with the intent of destroying vermin` IS `it is a gin or trap of the kind usually set to destroy vermin`
            `it was set, placed or continued from sunset to sunrise in a dwelling house for the protection of the dwelling house` IS `it was set from sunset to sunrise in a dwelling house to protect it`

§§ `Railways and carriages`

-- s 32. Unlawfully and maliciously putting something on a railway, removing a rail, turning points, or interfering with signals,
-- or doing any other thing, with intent to endanger the safety of a person on the railway.
@export Could I have broken section 32 by placing an obstruction on a railway or interfering with its signals?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I put wood, stone or another thing on or across a railway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you put or throw upon or across a railway wood, stone or other matter or thing?
      `I took up or moved a rail, sleeper or other part of a railway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you take up, remove or displace a rail, sleeper or other matter or thing belonging to a railway?
      `I turned, moved or diverted points or other machinery of a railway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you turn, move or divert points or other machinery belonging to a railway?
      `I made, showed, hid or removed a signal or light on or near a railway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you make or show, hide or remove a signal or light upon or near to a railway?
      `I did or caused to be done some other thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you do, or cause to be done, any other matter or thing?
      `I meant to endanger the safety of someone on the railway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to endanger the safety of a person travelling or being upon the railway?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 32 by placing an obstruction on a railway or interfering with its signals` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 32: placing an obstruction on a railway, with intent to endanger` c) `s 32: placing an obstruction on a railway, with intent to endanger`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 32` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `put or threw upon or across a railway wood, stone or other matter or thing` IS `I put wood, stone or another thing on or across a railway`
            `took up, removed or displaced a rail, sleeper or other matter or thing belonging to a railway` IS `I took up or moved a rail, sleeper or other part of a railway`
            `turned, moved or diverted points or other machinery belonging to a railway` IS `I turned, moved or diverted points or other machinery of a railway`
            `made or showed, hid or removed a signal or light upon or near to a railway` IS `I made, showed, hid or removed a signal or light on or near a railway`
            `did or caused to be done any other matter or thing` IS `I did or caused to be done some other thing`
            `intended to endanger the safety of a person travelling or being upon the railway` IS `I meant to endanger the safety of someone on the railway`

-- s 33. Unlawfully and maliciously throwing, or causing to fall or strike, wood, stone or another thing at, against, into or upon an engine,
-- tender, carriage or truck, with intent to injure or endanger the safety of a person in it or in the same train.
@export Could I have broken section 33 by throwing something at a railway carriage?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `my act was malicious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did malicious? (The Act uses the word "maliciously" and does not define it.)
      `I threw, or caused to fall or strike, something at or into a railway engine, tender, carriage or truck` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you throw, or cause to fall or strike, at, against, into or upon an engine, tender, carriage or truck used on a railway, wood, stone or another matter or thing?
      `I meant to injure someone in it or in the same train` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to injure a person in or upon that engine, tender, carriage or truck, or another in the same train?
      `I meant to endanger the safety of someone in it or in the same train` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to endanger the safety of a person in or upon that engine, tender, carriage or truck, or another in the same train?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 33 by throwing something at a railway carriage` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 33: casting a thing at a railway carriage, with intent` c) `s 33: casting a thing at a railway carriage, with intent`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 33` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `maliciously` IS `my act was malicious`
            `threw, or caused to fall or strike, at, against, into or upon an engine, tender, carriage or truck used upon a railway, wood, stone or another matter or thing` IS `I threw, or caused to fall or strike, something at or into a railway engine, tender, carriage or truck`
            `intended to injure a person in or upon that engine, tender, carriage or truck, or another in the same train` IS `I meant to injure someone in it or in the same train`
            `intended to endanger the safety of a person in or upon that engine, tender, carriage or truck, or another in the same train` IS `I meant to endanger the safety of someone in it or in the same train`

-- s 34. By any unlawful act, or by wilful omission or neglect, endangering or causing to be endangered the safety of a person on a railway,
-- or aiding or assisting in so doing. See NOTES.md fork 34a: "wilful" is read as attaching to the omission or neglect only.
@export Could I have broken section 34 by endangering railway passengers through an unlawful act or neglect?
GIVEN `I did an unlawful act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you do an unlawful act?
      `I wilfully omitted or neglected to do something` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you wilfully omit or neglect to do something?
      `I endangered, or caused to be endangered, the safety of a person on a railway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you endanger, or cause to be endangered, the safety of a person conveyed or being in or upon a railway?
      `I aided or assisted in endangering that safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you aid or assist in endangering that safety?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 34 by endangering railway passengers through an unlawful act or neglect` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 34: endangering railway passengers` c) `s 34: endangering railway passengers`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 34` WITH
            `did an unlawful act` IS `I did an unlawful act`
            `wilfully omitted or neglected to do something` IS `I wilfully omitted or neglected to do something`
            `endangered, or caused to be endangered, the safety of a person conveyed or being in or upon a railway` IS `I endangered, or caused to be endangered, the safety of a person on a railway`
            `aided or assisted in endangering that safety` IS `I aided or assisted in endangering that safety`

-- s 35. A person in charge of a carriage or vehicle who, by wanton or furious driving or racing, or other wilful misconduct or wilful neglect,
-- does or causes bodily harm to a person.
@export Could I have broken section 35 by causing bodily harm through furious driving or wilful misconduct?
GIVEN `I had the charge of a carriage or vehicle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have the charge of a carriage or vehicle?
      `I drove wantonly or furiously, or raced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you drive wantonly or furiously, or race?
      `I committed other wilful misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you commit other wilful misconduct?
      `I was guilty of wilful neglect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you guilty of wilful neglect?
      `I did, or caused to be done, bodily harm to a person by it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you do, or cause to be done, bodily harm to a person by it?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 35 by causing bodily harm through furious driving or wilful misconduct` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 35: bodily harm by furious driving or wilful misconduct` c) `s 35: bodily harm by furious driving or wilful misconduct`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 35` WITH
            `had the charge of a carriage or vehicle` IS `I had the charge of a carriage or vehicle`
            `drove wantonly or furiously, or raced` IS `I drove wantonly or furiously, or raced`
            `committed other wilful misconduct` IS `I committed other wilful misconduct`
            `was guilty of wilful neglect` IS `I was guilty of wilful neglect`
            `did or caused to be done bodily harm to a person by it` IS `I did, or caused to be done, bodily harm to a person by it`

§§ `Assaults`

-- s 36. By threats or force obstructing a minister in divine service or a burial; or striking, offering violence to, or arresting on civil process
-- a minister who is engaged in those duties or, to the offender's knowledge, about to be or going to or returning from them.
@export Could I have broken section 36 by obstructing, striking or arresting a clergyman or other minister?
GIVEN `I obstructed a minister at divine service by threats or force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By threats or force, did you obstruct or prevent (or try to obstruct or prevent) a clergyman or other minister in or from celebrating divine service or otherwise officiating in a place of divine worship?
      `I obstructed a minister at a burial by threats or force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By threats or force, did you obstruct or prevent (or try to obstruct or prevent) a clergyman or other minister in or from the performance of their duty in the lawful burial of the dead in a churchyard or other burial place?
      `I struck or offered violence to a minister` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you strike or offer violence to a clergyman or other minister?
      `I arrested a minister on civil process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you arrest a clergyman or other minister, upon civil process or under the pretence of executing civil process?
      `the minister was engaged in those duties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the minister engaged in those rites or duties?
      `I knew the minister was about to engage in those duties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the minister about to engage in those rites or duties, to your knowledge?
      `I knew the minister was going to or returning from those duties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the minister going to perform those rites or duties, or returning from performing them, to your knowledge?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 36 by obstructing, striking or arresting a minister` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 36: obstructing or assaulting a clergyman or other minister` c) `s 36: obstructing or assaulting a clergyman or other minister`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 36` WITH
            `by threats or force obstructed or prevented, or endeavoured to obstruct or prevent, a clergyman or other minister in or from celebrating divine service or otherwise officiating in a place of divine worship` IS `I obstructed a minister at divine service by threats or force`
            `by threats or force obstructed or prevented, or endeavoured to obstruct or prevent, a clergyman or other minister in or from the performance of their duty in the lawful burial of the dead in a churchyard or other burial place` IS `I obstructed a minister at a burial by threats or force`
            `struck or offered violence to a clergyman or other minister` IS `I struck or offered violence to a minister`
            `arrested a clergyman or other minister, upon civil process or under the pretence of executing civil process` IS `I arrested a minister on civil process`
            `the minister was engaged in those rites or duties` IS `the minister was engaged in those duties`
            `the minister was about to engage in those rites or duties, to the knowledge of the person` IS `I knew the minister was about to engage in those duties`
            `the minister was going to perform those rites or duties or returning from performing them, to the knowledge of the person` IS `I knew the minister was going to or returning from those duties`

-- s 37. Assaulting and striking or wounding a magistrate, officer or other person lawfully authorized, in or on account of the exercise
-- of their duty in or concerning the preservation of a vessel in distress or of a wreck.
@export Could I have broken section 37 by assaulting a person preserving a vessel or wreck?
GIVEN `I assaulted and struck a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you assault and strike a person?
      `I assaulted and wounded a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you assault and wound a person?
      `the person was a magistrate, officer or other person lawfully authorized` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person a magistrate, officer or other person lawfully authorized?
      `it was on account of their duty to preserve a vessel in distress or a wreck` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it in or on account of the exercise of their duty in or concerning the preservation of a vessel in distress, or of a vessel, goods or effects wrecked, stranded, cast on shore or lying under water?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 37 by assaulting a person preserving a vessel or wreck` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 37: assaulting a person preserving a vessel or wreck` c) `s 37: assaulting a person preserving a vessel or wreck`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 37` WITH
            `assaulted and struck a person` IS `I assaulted and struck a person`
            `assaulted and wounded a person` IS `I assaulted and wounded a person`
            `the person was a magistrate, officer or other person lawfully authorized` IS `the person was a magistrate, officer or other person lawfully authorized`
            `it was in or on account of the exercise of their duty in or concerning the preservation of a vessel in distress, or of a vessel, goods or effects wrecked, stranded, cast on shore or lying under water` IS `it was on account of their duty to preserve a vessel in distress or a wreck`

-- s 38. Assaulting a person with intent to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of oneself or of another for any offence.
-- The text opens with ". . ." so words are missing; see NOTES.md fork 38a.
@export Could I have broken section 38 by assaulting someone to resist or prevent a lawful arrest?
GIVEN `I assaulted a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you assault a person?
      `I meant to resist or prevent my own lawful arrest or detention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to resist or prevent your own lawful apprehension or detainer for an offence?
      `I meant to resist or prevent someone else's lawful arrest or detention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of another person for an offence?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 38 by assaulting someone to resist or prevent a lawful arrest` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 38: assault with intent to resist or prevent lawful apprehension` c) `s 38: assault with intent to resist or prevent lawful apprehension`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 38` WITH
            `assaulted a person` IS `I assaulted a person`
            `intended to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of themselves for an offence` IS `I meant to resist or prevent my own lawful arrest or detention`
            `intended to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of another person for an offence` IS `I meant to resist or prevent someone else's lawful arrest or detention`

-- s 39. Beating, or using violence or a threat of violence to, a person, with intent to deter them from, or compel them to, buying or selling
-- wheat, grain, flour, meal, malt or potatoes in a market; or to a person in charge of such goods on the way, with intent to stop their conveyance.
@export Could I have broken section 39 by using violence to obstruct the sale or carriage of grain?
GIVEN `I beat a person, or used violence or a threat of violence to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you beat a person, or use violence or a threat of violence to a person?
      `I meant to deter or hinder them from, or compel them to, buying or selling grain, flour, meal, malt or potatoes in a market` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to deter or hinder them from buying, selling or otherwise disposing of, or to compel them to buy, sell or otherwise dispose of, wheat, other grain, flour, meal, malt or potatoes in a market or other place?
      `the person had the care of such goods on the way to or from a city, market town or other place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person have the care or charge of wheat, other grain, flour, meal, malt or potatoes, whilst on the way to or from a city, market town or other place?
      `I meant to stop the conveyance of the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to stop the conveyance of them?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 39 by using violence to obstruct the sale or carriage of grain` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 39: violence to obstruct the sale or conveyance of grain` c) `s 39: violence to obstruct the sale or conveyance of grain`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 39` WITH
            `beat, or used violence or a threat of violence to, a person` IS `I beat a person, or used violence or a threat of violence to them`
            `intended to deter or hinder them from buying, selling or otherwise disposing of, or to compel them to buy, sell or otherwise dispose of, wheat, other grain, flour, meal, malt or potatoes in a market or other place` IS `I meant to deter or hinder them from, or compel them to, buying or selling grain, flour, meal, malt or potatoes in a market`
            `the person had the care or charge of wheat, other grain, flour, meal, malt or potatoes, whilst on the way to or from a city, market town or other place` IS `the person had the care of such goods on the way to or from a city, market town or other place`
            `intended to stop the conveyance of it` IS `I meant to stop the conveyance of the goods`

-- s 39, proviso. A person punished under this section is not to be punished for the same offence under any other law.
-- The person states whether they have already been punished under section 39.
@export Once punished under section 39, can I be punished again for the same offence under another law (s 39)?
GIVEN `I have been punished under section 39` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you already been punished for this offence under section 39?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Once punished under section 39 can I be punished again under another law` IS
    `s 39: the person cannot be punished for the same offence under any other law` `I have been punished under section 39`

-- s 40. Unlawfully and with force hindering a seaman, keelman or caster from working; or beating or using violence to one with intent to hinder
-- them from working. "Unlawfully" is in the first limb only.
@export Could I have broken section 40 by using violence to hinder a seaman, keelman or caster?
GIVEN `I acted unlawfully` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `I hindered or prevented a seaman, keelman or caster from working, with force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc With force, did you hinder or prevent a seaman, keelman or caster from working at or exercising their lawful trade, business or occupation?
      `I beat or used violence to a seaman, keelman or caster` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you beat or use any violence to a seaman, keelman or caster?
      `I meant to hinder or prevent them from working` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to hinder or prevent them from working at or exercising their trade, business or occupation?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 40 by using violence to hinder a seaman, keelman or caster` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 40: violence to hinder a seaman, keelman or caster` c) `s 40: violence to hinder a seaman, keelman or caster`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 40` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `I acted unlawfully`
            `with force hindered or prevented a seaman, keelman or caster from working at or exercising their lawful trade, business or occupation` IS `I hindered or prevented a seaman, keelman or caster from working, with force`
            `beat or used violence to a seaman, keelman or caster` IS `I beat or used violence to a seaman, keelman or caster`
            `intended to hinder or prevent them from working at or exercising their trade, business or occupation` IS `I meant to hinder or prevent them from working`

-- s 40, proviso, word for word the same as section 39's.
@export Once punished under section 40, can I be punished again for the same offence under another law (s 40)?
GIVEN `I have been punished under section 40` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you already been punished for this offence under section 40?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Once punished under section 40 can I be punished again under another law` IS
    `s 40: the person cannot be punished for the same offence under any other law` `I have been punished under section 40`

-- s 42. Two justices of the peace, on complaint by or for the party aggrieved, may hear and determine a case of unlawful assault or battery.
-- The heading says 5 l. but the operative words say 200 pounds; the words govern (NOTES.md fork 42a). This is the justices' power and the
-- person's liability to it; the limits of that power are in the two questions that follow.
@export Could two justices hear a complaint against me of assault or battery, and what is the most they can order (s 42)?
GIVEN `I unlawfully assaulted or beat another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you accused of unlawfully assaulting or beating another person?
      `the party aggrieved, or someone for them, made a complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a complaint made by or on behalf of the party aggrieved?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could two justices hear a complaint against me of assault or battery` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 42: two justices may hear and determine a common assault or battery` c) `s 42: common assault or battery`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 42` WITH
            `unlawfully assaulted or beat another person` IS `I unlawfully assaulted or beat another person`
            `a complaint was made by or on behalf of the party aggrieved` IS `the party aggrieved, or someone for them, made a complaint`

-- s 42. The most two justices can order as a fine: the fine together with any costs may not exceed 200 pounds.
@export Is a fine of this size, with costs, within what two justices can order for assault or battery (s 42)?
GIVEN `the fine in pounds` IS A NUMBER @desc How much is the fine, in pounds?
      `the costs in pounds` IS A NUMBER @desc How much are the costs ordered with it, in pounds? (0 if none)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this fine with costs within what two justices can order` IS
    `s 42: the fine together with the costs is within the maximum` `the fine in pounds` `the costs in pounds`

-- s 42. The most two justices can order as imprisonment, directly or for not paying: 6 months.
@export Is a term of imprisonment of this length within what two justices can order for assault or battery (s 42)?
GIVEN `the imprisonment in months` IS A NUMBER @desc How many months of imprisonment are ordered, whether directly or for not paying a fine and costs?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this imprisonment within what two justices can order` IS
    `s 42: the imprisonment is within the maximum` `the imprisonment in months`

-- Situation-only group (sections 44 and 45): the form may ask "Did two justices hear a complaint against you of assault or
-- battery?" before showing them.
-- s 44. Where the justices dismiss the complaint on one of three grounds, they must forthwith make out and deliver a certificate to the
-- person complained against. That is the person's claim; the justices' duty is its correlative (no seat for the justices).
-- The justices' findings are facts about the hearing that the person can state.
@export Am I entitled to a certificate of dismissal (s 44)?
GIVEN `a complaint was brought against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a complaint of assault or battery brought against you before the justices by or for the party aggrieved?
      `the justices heard the case on its merits` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the justices hear the case on its merits?
      `the justices decided the offence was not proved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the justices decide that the offence was not proved?
      `the justices found what I did justified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the justices find the assault or battery justified?
      `the justices found it so trifling as not to merit punishment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the justices find the assault or battery so trifling as not to merit any punishment?
      `the justices dismissed the complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the justices dismiss the complaint?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I entitled to a certificate of dismissal` IF
    `s 44: a certificate of dismissal is owed to the person complained against` d
    WHERE
        d MEANS `Dismissal under section 44` WITH
            `a complaint was preferred by or on behalf of the party aggrieved under section 42 or 43` IS `a complaint was brought against me`
            `the justices heard the case upon the merits` IS `the justices heard the case on its merits`
            `the justices deemed the offence not proved` IS `the justices decided the offence was not proved`
            `the justices found the assault or battery justified` IS `the justices found what I did justified`
            `the justices found the assault or battery so trifling as not to merit any punishment` IS `the justices found it so trifling as not to merit punishment`
            `the justices dismissed the complaint` IS `the justices dismissed the complaint`

-- s 44. By when the certificate is owed. The text says "forthwith" and gives no number of days.
@export By when must the justices give me the certificate of dismissal (s 44)?
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `By when must the justices give me the certificate of dismissal` IS
    `s 44: when the certificate is due`

-- s 45. The person's immunity: once the certificate is obtained, or the conviction's whole amount paid or its imprisonment suffered,
-- no further civil or criminal proceedings for the same cause. The correlative disability is on the party aggrieved (no seat for them).
@export Am I protected from any further civil or criminal proceedings for the same cause (s 45)?
GIVEN `a complaint was brought against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a complaint of assault or battery brought against you before the justices by or for the party aggrieved?
      `I obtained a certificate of dismissal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you obtain a certificate of dismissal?
      `I was convicted and paid the whole amount` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted and have you paid the whole amount adjudged to be paid?
      `I was convicted and served the imprisonment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted and have you suffered the imprisonment awarded?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I protected from any further proceedings for the same cause` IF
    `s 45: the person is released from all further proceedings, civil or criminal, for the same cause` r
    WHERE
        r MEANS `Release under section 45` WITH
            `a complaint of the kind in section 44 was preferred by or on behalf of the party aggrieved` IS `a complaint was brought against me`
            `the person obtained such a certificate` IS `I obtained a certificate of dismissal`
            `the person, having been convicted, paid the whole amount adjudged to be paid` IS `I was convicted and paid the whole amount`
            `the person, having been convicted, suffered the imprisonment awarded` IS `I was convicted and served the imprisonment`

-- s 47. An assault occasioning actual bodily harm. The text defines neither "assault" nor "actual bodily harm".
@export Could I have broken section 47 by an assault that caused actual bodily harm?
GIVEN `I assaulted a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you assault a person?
      `the assault caused actual bodily harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the assault cause actual bodily harm?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 47 by an assault that caused actual bodily harm` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 47: assault occasioning actual bodily harm` c) `s 47: assault occasioning actual bodily harm`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 47` WITH
            `assaulted a person` IS `I assaulted a person`
            `the assault occasioned actual bodily harm` IS `the assault caused actual bodily harm`

§§ `Bigamy`

-- s 57. A married person who marries another during the life of the former husband or wife, in England or Ireland or elsewhere,
-- unless one of four exceptions applies: a second marriage contracted outside England and Ireland by a non-subject; a spouse continually
-- absent for seven years and not known to be living; a divorce; a former marriage declared void.
@export Could my second marriage amount to bigamy (s 57)?
GIVEN `I was married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you married?
      `I married another person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you marry another person?
      `my husband or wife was still living when I did` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your husband or wife still living when you did?
      `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the second marriage take place in England or Ireland?
      `I am a subject of Her Majesty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a subject of Her Majesty?
      `the complete years my husband or wife had been continually absent` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many complete years had your husband or wife been continually absent from you at the time of the second marriage? (0 if not absent)
      `I knew my husband or wife to be living within that time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you know your husband or wife to be living at any time within that period of absence?
      `I had been divorced from the first marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of the second marriage, had you been divorced from the bond of the first marriage?
      `the first marriage had been declared void by a court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had your former marriage been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could my second marriage amount to bigamy` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 57: bigamy` c) `s 57: bigamy`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 57` WITH
            `the person was married` IS `I was married`
            `the person married another person` IS `I married another person`
            `the former husband or wife was then living` IS `my husband or wife was still living when I did`
            `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland` IS `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland`
            `the person is a subject of Her Majesty` IS `I am a subject of Her Majesty`
            `the number of complete years the husband or wife had been continually absent` IS `the complete years my husband or wife had been continually absent`
            `the person had known the husband or wife to be living within that time` IS `I knew my husband or wife to be living within that time`
            `the person had been divorced from the bond of the first marriage` IS `I had been divorced from the first marriage`
            `the former marriage had been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction` IS `the first marriage had been declared void by a court`

-- s 57, first exception: a second marriage contracted elsewhere than in England and Ireland by a person who is not a subject of Her Majesty.
@export Am I excused from a bigamy charge because I married abroad and am not a subject of Her Majesty (s 57)?
GIVEN `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the second marriage take place in England or Ireland?
      `I am a subject of Her Majesty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a subject of Her Majesty?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I excused from bigamy because I married abroad and am not a subject` IS
    `s 57: exception for a second marriage contracted abroad by a person who is not a subject` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 57` WITH
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person was married` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person married another person` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the former husband or wife was then living` IS FALSE
            `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland` IS `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland`
            `the person is a subject of Her Majesty` IS `I am a subject of Her Majesty`
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the number of complete years the husband or wife had been continually absent` IS 0
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person had known the husband or wife to be living within that time` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person had been divorced from the bond of the first marriage` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the former marriage had been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction` IS FALSE

-- s 57, second exception: seven years' continual absence and the person not knowing the spouse to be living within that time.
-- Seven is enough; six is not.
@export Am I excused from a bigamy charge because my husband or wife had been absent for seven years and I did not know they were living (s 57)?
GIVEN `the complete years my husband or wife had been continually absent` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many complete years had your husband or wife been continually absent from you at the time of the second marriage? (0 if not absent)
      `I knew my husband or wife to be living within that time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you know your husband or wife to be living at any time within that period of absence?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I excused from bigamy because my spouse had been absent seven years` IS
    `s 57: exception for a spouse continually absent seven years and not known to be living` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 57` WITH
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person was married` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person married another person` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the former husband or wife was then living` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person is a subject of Her Majesty` IS FALSE
            `the number of complete years the husband or wife had been continually absent` IS `the complete years my husband or wife had been continually absent`
            `the person had known the husband or wife to be living within that time` IS `I knew my husband or wife to be living within that time`
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person had been divorced from the bond of the first marriage` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the former marriage had been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction` IS FALSE

-- s 57, third exception: the person had been divorced from the bond of the first marriage at the time of the second marriage.
@export Am I excused from a bigamy charge because I was divorced from the first marriage (s 57)?
GIVEN `I had been divorced from the first marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of the second marriage, had you been divorced from the bond of the first marriage?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I excused from bigamy because I was divorced` IS
    `s 57: exception for a person divorced from the first marriage` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 57` WITH
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person was married` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person married another person` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the former husband or wife was then living` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person is a subject of Her Majesty` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the number of complete years the husband or wife had been continually absent` IS 0
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person had known the husband or wife to be living within that time` IS FALSE
            `the person had been divorced from the bond of the first marriage` IS `I had been divorced from the first marriage`
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the former marriage had been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction` IS FALSE

-- s 57, fourth exception: the former marriage had been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction.
@export Am I excused from a bigamy charge because my former marriage had been declared void (s 57)?
GIVEN `the first marriage had been declared void by a court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had your former marriage been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I excused from bigamy because my former marriage had been declared void` IS
    `s 57: exception for a former marriage declared void` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 57` WITH
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person was married` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person married another person` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the former husband or wife was then living` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the second marriage took place in England or Ireland` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person is a subject of Her Majesty` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the number of complete years the husband or wife had been continually absent` IS 0
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person had known the husband or wife to be living within that time` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: this exception does not read this field.
            `the person had been divorced from the bond of the first marriage` IS FALSE
            `the former marriage had been declared void by the sentence of a court of competent jurisdiction` IS `the first marriage had been declared void by a court`

§§ `Attempts to procure abortion, and concealing the birth of a child`

-- s 58. Limb one: a woman with child, intending her own miscarriage, unlawfully taking a noxious thing or using an instrument on herself.
-- Limb two: any person, intending the miscarriage of any woman (with child or not), unlawfully giving her a noxious thing or using an instrument.
-- Whether an act is "unlawful" depends on law outside this Act, which is not brought in; the person states it.
@export Could I have broken section 58 by taking, giving or using something to procure a miscarriage?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `I was a woman with child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you a woman with child at the time?
      `I meant to procure my own miscarriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to procure your own miscarriage?
      `I took a poison or other noxious thing myself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you administer to yourself a poison or other noxious thing?
      `I used an instrument or other means on myself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you use an instrument or other means on yourself?
      `I meant to procure the miscarriage of a woman, whether or not she was with child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to procure the miscarriage of a woman, whether or not she was with child?
      `I gave that woman, or caused her to take, a poison or other noxious thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you administer to that woman, or cause to be taken by her, a poison or other noxious thing?
      `I used an instrument or other means on that woman` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you use an instrument or other means on that woman?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 58 by taking, giving or using something to procure a miscarriage` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 58: administering drugs or using instruments to procure abortion` c) `s 58: administering drugs or using instruments to procure abortion`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 58` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `the person is a woman who was with child` IS `I was a woman with child`
            `intended to procure her own miscarriage` IS `I meant to procure my own miscarriage`
            `administered to herself a poison or other noxious thing` IS `I took a poison or other noxious thing myself`
            `used an instrument or other means whatsoever upon herself` IS `I used an instrument or other means on myself`
            `intended to procure the miscarriage of a woman, whether or not she was with child` IS `I meant to procure the miscarriage of a woman, whether or not she was with child`
            `administered to that woman, or caused to be taken by her, a poison or other noxious thing` IS `I gave that woman, or caused her to take, a poison or other noxious thing`
            `used an instrument or other means whatsoever upon that woman` IS `I used an instrument or other means on that woman`

-- s 59. Unlawfully supplying or procuring a poison, instrument or thing, knowing it is intended to be unlawfully used to procure the miscarriage
-- of a woman, whether or not she is with child. The person need not intend the miscarriage; they must know of the intended use.
@export Could I have broken section 59 by supplying something to be used to procure a miscarriage?
GIVEN `my act was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was what you did unlawful, that is, done without lawful justification or excuse?
      `I supplied or procured a poison, instrument or other thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you supply or procure a poison or other noxious thing, or an instrument or thing whatsoever?
      `I knew it was intended to be unlawfully used to procure a miscarriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you know that it was intended to be unlawfully used or employed with intent to procure the miscarriage of a woman, whether or not she was with child?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 59 by supplying something to be used to procure a miscarriage` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 59: supplying drugs or instruments to procure abortion` c) `s 59: supplying drugs or instruments to procure abortion`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 59` WITH
            `unlawfully` IS `my act was unlawful`
            `supplied or procured a poison or other noxious thing, or an instrument or thing whatsoever` IS `I supplied or procured a poison, instrument or other thing`
            `knew that it was intended to be unlawfully used or employed with intent to procure the miscarriage of a woman, whether or not she was with child` IS `I knew it was intended to be unlawfully used to procure a miscarriage`

-- s 60. Where a woman is delivered of a child, every person who by any secret disposition of the dead body of the child endeavours to conceal
-- the birth, whether the child died before, at or after its birth. The offender may be the woman or anyone else.
@export Could I have broken section 60 by secretly disposing of a child's body to conceal its birth?
GIVEN `a woman was delivered of a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a woman delivered of a child?
      `I made a secret disposition of the dead body of the child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you make a secret disposition of the dead body of the child?
      `I tried by that to conceal the birth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you endeavour by that to conceal the birth of the child?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could I have broken section 60 by secretly disposing of a child's body to conceal its birth` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 60: concealing the birth of a child` c) `s 60: concealing the birth of a child`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 60` WITH
            `a woman was delivered of a child` IS `a woman was delivered of a child`
            `the person made a secret disposition of the dead body of the child` IS `I made a secret disposition of the dead body of the child`
            `the person endeavoured thereby to conceal the birth of the child` IS `I tried by that to conceal the birth`
            -- Placeholder: the rule does not read this field; section 60 reaches "every person", the woman or anyone else.
            `the person is the woman who was delivered` IS TRUE

§§ `Gunpowder, and searches for it`

-- s 64. Knowingly having or making gunpowder, an explosive or dangerous thing, a machine, engine, instrument or thing, with intent
-- to commit any of the felonies of this Act, or to enable another to. The person chooses which offence of the Act they intended; whether
-- the Act calls that offence a felony is worked out from the Act's own sections, never asked.
@export Could having or making gunpowder or a dangerous thing amount to an offence under section 64?
GIVEN `I knowingly had or made gunpowder, an explosive or dangerous thing, or a machine, engine, instrument or thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you knowingly have in your possession, or make or manufacture, gunpowder, an explosive substance, a dangerous or noxious thing, or any machine, engine, instrument or thing?
      `I meant to use it to commit an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you intend to commit an offence by means of it?
      `I made it to enable someone else to commit an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have it, or make it, for the purpose of enabling another person to commit an offence?
      `the offence is one of this Act's offences` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you or the other person intended one of the offences of this Act (see the list of offences)?
      `the offence intended` IS AN Offence @desc Which offence of this Act was intended?
GIVETH A `What the Act makes of it`
DECIDE `Could having or making gunpowder or a dangerous thing amount to an offence under section 64` IS
    `what the Act makes of an offence` (`s 64: making or having gunpowder or a dangerous thing to commit a felony of this Act` c)
                                        `s 64: making or having gunpowder or a dangerous thing to commit a felony of this Act`
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Conduct under section 64` WITH
            `knowingly had in possession, or made or manufactured, gunpowder, an explosive substance, a dangerous or noxious thing, or a machine, engine, instrument or thing` IS `I knowingly had or made gunpowder, an explosive or dangerous thing, or a machine, engine, instrument or thing`
            `intended by means of it to commit an offence` IS `I meant to use it to commit an offence`
            `made it for the purpose of enabling another person to commit an offence` IS `I made it to enable someone else to commit an offence`
            -- Derived: the Act's own word for the offence intended; an offence outside this Act has no word in it.
            `how the text classifies the offence intended` IS `the classification of the offence intended`
        `the classification of the offence intended` MEANS
            IF `the offence is one of this Act's offences`
            THEN `the text calls the offence` `the offence intended`
            ELSE `no classification stated`

-- Situation-only group (section 65): the form may ask "Has a search warrant been applied for, or issued, against your premises, carriage or
-- vessel?" before showing it.

-- s 65. A justice of the peace, on reasonable cause assigned upon oath, may issue a warrant to search a place for gunpowder or other
-- dangerous things suspected to be made, kept or carried to commit a felony of the Act. That is the justice's power and the person's liability.
-- Whether the cause is "reasonable" is the justice's judgment, taken here as a fact about the application.
@export Can a justice of the peace issue a warrant to search my premises, carriage or vessel (s 65)?
GIVEN `gunpowder or a dangerous thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried for a felony of the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is such a thing suspected to be made, kept or carried for the purpose of being used in committing one of the felonies of the Act?
      `cause was assigned upon oath` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was cause assigned upon oath by a person?
      `the justice accepts the cause as reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the justice of the peace accept that the cause assigned is reasonable?
      `the place is one the section lists` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the place a house, mill, magazine, storehouse, warehouse, shop, cellar, yard, wharf or other place, or a carriage, waggon, cart, ship, boat or vessel, in which the thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can a justice of the peace issue a warrant to search my premises, carriage or vessel` IS
    `s 65: a justice of the peace may issue a warrant to search the place` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Search under section 65` WITH
            `such a thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried for the purpose of being used in committing a felony mentioned in the Act` IS `gunpowder or a dangerous thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried for a felony of the Act`
            `cause was assigned upon oath by a person` IS `cause was assigned upon oath`
            `the justice of the peace accepts that the cause assigned is reasonable` IS `the justice accepts the cause as reasonable`
            `the place is a house, mill, magazine, storehouse, warehouse, shop, cellar, yard, wharf, or other place, or a carriage, waggon, cart, ship, boat or vessel, in which the thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried` IS `the place is one the section lists`
            -- Placeholder: the warrant rule does not read this field.
            `the thing was found upon the search` IS FALSE
            -- Placeholder: the warrant rule does not read this field.
            `the person executing the warrant has good cause to suspect it to be intended to be used in committing such a felony` IS FALSE

-- s 65. The person executing the warrant may seize, remove and detain what is found that they have good cause to suspect is intended for
-- such a felony, and its containers. The powers borrowed from the 1860 Act are not in this text and are not encoded.
@export Can what is found on a search be seized, removed and detained (s 65)?
GIVEN `gunpowder or a dangerous thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried for a felony of the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is such a thing suspected to be made, kept or carried for the purpose of being used in committing one of the felonies of the Act?
      `cause was assigned upon oath` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was cause assigned upon oath by a person?
      `the justice accepts the cause as reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the justice of the peace accept that the cause assigned is reasonable?
      `the place is one the section lists` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the place a house, mill, magazine, storehouse, warehouse, shop, cellar, yard, wharf or other place, or a carriage, waggon, cart, ship, boat or vessel, in which the thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried?
      `the thing was found on the search` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the thing found upon the search?
      `the person searching has good cause to suspect it is intended for such a felony` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person executing the warrant have good cause to suspect it to be intended to be used in committing such a felony?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can what is found on a search be seized, removed and detained` IS
    `s 65: the thing and its receptacles may be seized, removed and detained` c
    WHERE
        c MEANS `Search under section 65` WITH
            `such a thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried for the purpose of being used in committing a felony mentioned in the Act` IS `gunpowder or a dangerous thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried for a felony of the Act`
            `cause was assigned upon oath by a person` IS `cause was assigned upon oath`
            `the justice of the peace accepts that the cause assigned is reasonable` IS `the justice accepts the cause as reasonable`
            `the place is a house, mill, magazine, storehouse, warehouse, shop, cellar, yard, wharf, or other place, or a carriage, waggon, cart, ship, boat or vessel, in which the thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried` IS `the place is one the section lists`
            `the thing was found upon the search` IS `the thing was found on the search`
            `the person executing the warrant has good cause to suspect it to be intended to be used in committing such a felony` IS `the person searching has good cause to suspect it is intended for such a felony`

§§ `Offences on the water, and where the Act reaches`

-- s 68. Offences of the Act (those that are indictable) committed within the jurisdiction of the Admiralty of England or Ireland carry the
-- same punishments as if committed on land. The person chooses the offence; whether it is indictable is taken from the Act's own words
-- (sections 16 and 47 say "on indictment"; 39, 40 and 42 say "before two justices") and, where the Act says nothing, from what the person states.
@export Am I liable to the same punishment for an offence on the water within the Admiralty's jurisdiction as for one on land (s 68)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence @desc Which offence of this Act is it?
      `it was committed within the jurisdiction of the Admiralty of England or Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it committed within the jurisdiction of the Admiralty of England or Ireland?
      `it is indictable, where the Act does not say` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the Act does not say how this offence is tried, is it indictable?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I liable to the same punishment for an offence within the Admiralty's jurisdiction` IF
    `s 68: the person is liable to the same punishments as if the offence had been committed upon the land` o
    WHERE
        o MEANS `Offence within the Admiralty jurisdiction` WITH
            -- Derived: the person chose it from the Act's own list of offences.
            `the offence is one mentioned in this Act` IS TRUE
            `how the text says the offence is tried` IS `the text says the offence is tried` `the offence`
            `the offence is indictable, where the text does not say` IS `it is indictable, where the Act does not say`
            `it was committed within the jurisdiction of the Admiralty of England or Ireland` IS `it was committed within the jurisdiction of the Admiralty of England or Ireland`

-- s 78. "Nothing in this Act contained shall extend to Scotland, except as herein-before otherwise expressly provided." The deposited text names
-- Scotland nowhere before section 78, so the input to the layer-1 rule is fixed at FALSE. The text carries ". . ." marks where words were repealed;
-- if one of them hid an express extension, this answer would change (NOTES.md fork 78a).
@export Does the Act reach what I did if I did it in Scotland (s 78)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act reach what I did in Scotland` IF
    `s 78: the Act extends to Scotland for this matter` FALSE

-- These two questions take the offence as a choice from the Act's own list, so they cover every offence at once.
§§ `The most that can follow, for any offence of the Act`

-- What the Act states for the offence: what it calls it, how it says it is tried, and the sentences, with the words "the text does not state"
-- wherever a term is hidden by ". . ." in the deposited text.
@export What is the most I could face if I am convicted of this offence?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence @desc Which offence of this Act are you convicted of, or accused of?
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `What is the most I could face if I am convicted of this offence` IS
    CONCAT "The Act calls it ", `the classification in words` (`the text calls the offence` `the offence`),
           "; how it is tried: ", `the mode of trial in words` (`the text says the offence is tried` `the offence`),
           "; the most it states: ", `the sentences in words` (`the sentences the text states for the offence` `the offence`)

-- Is the sentence I have been given, or am facing, within the most the Act states for the offence? The answer names one of four outcomes,
-- including the two where the deposited text gives no figure to compare with.
@export Is this custodial sentence within the most the Act states for the offence?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence @desc Which offence of this Act is it?
      `the kind of sentence` IS A `Kind of custodial sentence` @desc Is the sentence penal servitude or imprisonment?
      `the sentence is for life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the sentence for life?
      `the months` IS A NUMBER @desc If it is not for life, how many months is it? (0 if it is for life)
GIVETH A `What the text says of a proposed sentence`
DECIDE `Is this custodial sentence within the most the Act states for the offence` IS
    `a proposed custodial sentence measured against the text` `the offence` `the kind of sentence` `the sentence is for life` `the months`
